// shelf: admit · root bck.NEW-CMUT — RE-PORTED AS-IS 2026-09-07 (stage 4)
// ── BUCKET 2: re-ported as-is, still admitted, still a defect. The old port went
//    through `Box<dyn Fn() -> i64>`; upstream's `-> impl Fn() -> usize` is supported
//    directly and is admitted too, so the boxing was neither needed nor the cause.
// ── ROOT RE-CHECKED, AND IT IS NOT bck.C ("the closure boundary deposits nothing").
//    The Fn/FnMut/FnOnce distinction DOES constrain CONSUMPTION here — measured
//    2026-09-07, a `move` closure that consumes its capture and is called twice is
//    REFUSED ("use of moved value 'c': ... an `FnOnce` is consumed by the call and
//    cannot be called again"). What is unconstrained is MUTATION: nothing makes a
//    closure returned as `Fn` illegal for assigning to its own upvar. Separate arm,
//    hence a separate root id.
// Original path: tests/ui/borrowck/borrow-immutable-upvar-mutation-impl-trait.rs
// upstream commit: 4b0c9d76ae7d387229caea55cfa73c280b08b8a7
// upstream construct: lines 8-14 —
//     fn bar() -> impl Fn() -> usize {
//         let mut x = 0;
//         move || { x += 1; //~ ERROR cannot assign
//                   x }
//     }
// Modifications:
//   · usize -> i64; assert -> exit code (`fn main() -> i32`). NOTHING ELSE.
package borrow_immutable_upvar_mutation_impl_trait;
fn bar() -> impl Fn() -> i64 {
    let mut x: i64 = 0i64;
    return move || -> i64 { x += 1i64; return x; };
}
fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
